.Dd September 25, 2026
.Dt TZSETUP 8
.Os NextBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm tzsetup
.Nd set the time zone, and the network clock with it
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl n
.Op Ar zone
.Nm
.Fl l
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
sets the machine's time zone.
Run with no arguments it asks, in three screens \(em region, zone, then a
confirmation that also offers to keep the clock synchronised over the network.
Given a
.Ar zone
it sets it and says nothing, which is what a script or an installer wants.
.Pp
Setting the zone writes two files.
.Pa /etc/localtime
gets a
.Em copy
of the zone's file from
.Pa /usr/share/zoneinfo ,
not a symbolic link: a link into
.Pa /usr/share
would break a single-user boot, where only the root filesystem is mounted.
.Pa /var/db/zoneinfo
records the zone's name, so anything asking which zone this is gets an answer
without comparing file contents.
.Ss The network clock
The confirmation screen carries a checkbox:
.Pp
.Dl [x] Keep the clock synchronised over the network
.Pp
Ticked, it runs
.Li launchctl load -w
on
.Li org.nextbsd.ntpd ,
which persists across reboots through launchd's overrides database.
Unticked on a machine where the job is already running, it unloads it.
The box shows the job's current state when the screen opens, so leaving it
alone changes nothing.
.Pp
That job ships
.Li Disabled ,
so without this it is off on every fresh install and nothing says so.
Setting a time zone and keeping the clock right are one thought, which is why
they are one screen.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl l
List every zone in the table, one per line, and exit.
.It Fl n
Say what would be set without setting it.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/usr/share/zoneinfo/zone1970.tab" -compact
.It Pa /usr/share/zoneinfo
the zone files
.It Pa /usr/share/zoneinfo/zone1970.tab
the zone table;
.Pa zone.tab
is read where that is absent
.It Pa /usr/share/zoneinfo/iso3166.tab
country names, for the menu
.It Pa /etc/localtime
the chosen zone, copied
.It Pa /var/db/zoneinfo
its name
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Ask, in the terminal:
.Pp
.Dl # tzsetup
.Pp
Set one without asking, for a script:
.Pp
.Dl # tzsetup Europe/London
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr date 1 ,
.Xr launchctl 1 ,
.Xr ntpd 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Joseph Maloney
.Sh CAVEATS
A zone name indexes the filesystem, so one containing
.Ql ..
or a leading
.Ql /
is refused before it is used as a path.
.Pp
Without a terminal and without a
.Ar zone
argument there is nothing sensible to do, and
.Nm
says so rather than presenting a dialog nothing can drive.
